The `ScheduledTask` class wraps a `Runnable` with scheduling metadata. It implements `Delayed` so it can sit in a `DelayQueue`, and `Comparable` for priority ordering within the same time window.

```java
public class ScheduledTask implements Delayed, Comparable<ScheduledTask> {

    private final String taskId;
    private final Runnable command;
    private final int priority;          // lower = higher priority
    private long nextExecutionNanos;      // absolute nanoTime
    private final long periodNanos;       // 0 = one-shot, >0 = periodic
    private volatile boolean cancelled = false;

    public ScheduledTask(String taskId, Runnable command,
                         int priority, long delayMs, long periodMs) {
        this.taskId = taskId;
        this.command = command;
        this.priority = priority;
        this.nextExecutionNanos = System.nanoTime()
            + TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS.toNanos(delayMs);
        this.periodNanos = TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS.toNanos(periodMs);
    }

    @Override
    public long getDelay(TimeUnit unit) {
        return unit.convert(
            nextExecutionNanos - System.nanoTime(), TimeUnit.NANOSECONDS);
    }

    @Override
    public int compareTo(ScheduledTask other) {
        // Primary: execution time. Secondary: priority.
        int timeCmp = Long.compare(this.nextExecutionNanos,
                                    other.nextExecutionNanos);
        return timeCmp != 0 ? timeCmp
            : Integer.compare(this.priority, other.priority);
    }

    public boolean isPeriodic() { return periodNanos > 0; }

    public void advanceExecution() {
        nextExecutionNanos += periodNanos; // fixed-rate scheduling
    }

    public void cancel() { this.cancelled = true; }
    public boolean isCancelled() { return cancelled; }
    public Runnable getCommand() { return command; }
    public String getTaskId() { return taskId; }
}
```

Using `System.nanoTime()` instead of `System.currentTimeMillis()` avoids clock skew from NTP adjustments. Periodic tasks call `advanceExecution()` to set the next fire time, which implements **fixed-rate** scheduling. For **fixed-delay**, you would set `nextExecutionNanos = System.nanoTime() + periodNanos` after each run completes.

public class TaskScheduler {

    private final DelayQueue<ScheduledTask> queue = new DelayQueue<>();
    private final ExecutorService workerPool;
    private volatile boolean running = true;

    public TaskScheduler(int poolSize) {
        this.workerPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(poolSize);
        Thread dispatcher = new Thread(this::dispatchLoop,
                                        "scheduler-dispatcher");
        dispatcher.setDaemon(true);  // don't block JVM shutdown
        dispatcher.start();
    }

    public void schedule(ScheduledTask task) {
        queue.put(task);  // thread-safe, non-blocking
    }

    private void dispatchLoop() {
        while (running) {
            try {
                // Blocks until a task's delay expires
                ScheduledTask task = queue.take();

                if (task.isCancelled()) continue;

                workerPool.submit(() -> {
                    try {
                        task.getCommand().run();
                    } catch (Exception e) {
                        // Log but don't kill the scheduler
                        System.err.println("Task "
                            + task.getTaskId() + " failed: " + e);
                    } finally {
                        if (task.isPeriodic() && !task.isCancelled()) {
                            task.advanceExecution();
                            queue.put(task);  // re-enqueue
                        }
                    }
                });
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    public void shutdown() {
        running = false;
        workerPool.shutdown();
    }
}

public class ConnectionPool {

    private final ConcurrentLinkedDeque<PooledConnection> idle
        = new ConcurrentLinkedDeque<>();
    private final Set<PooledConnection> active
        = ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet();  // track active for leak detection
    private final Semaphore permits;       // bounds total connections
    private final String jdbcUrl;
    private final int maxSize;
    private final long borrowTimeoutMs;

    public ConnectionPool(String jdbcUrl, int maxSize,
                          long borrowTimeoutMs) {
        this.jdbcUrl = jdbcUrl;
        this.maxSize = maxSize;
        this.borrowTimeoutMs = borrowTimeoutMs;
        this.permits = new Semaphore(maxSize, true); // fair ordering
    }

    public Connection borrow() throws SQLException {
        try {
            // Block if all connections are in use
            if (!permits.tryAcquire(borrowTimeoutMs,
                                     TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
                throw new SQLException(
                    "Connection pool exhausted, waited "
                    + borrowTimeoutMs + "ms");
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            throw new SQLException("Interrupted waiting for connection");
        }

        PooledConnection conn = idle.pollFirst();

        // No idle connection available → create new one
        if (conn == null || !conn.isHealthy()) {
            conn = createNewConnection();
        }

        conn.markBorrowed();  // record checkout timestamp
        active.add(conn);
        return conn.getConnection();
    }

    public void release(Connection connection) {
        PooledConnection pooled = findPooled(connection);
        active.remove(pooled);
        pooled.markReturned();

        if (pooled.isHealthy()) {
            idle.addFirst(pooled);  // LIFO: reuse hot connections
        } else {
            pooled.closeQuietly();  // discard and allow new creation
        }
        permits.release();  // allow another thread to borrow
    }

    private PooledConnection createNewConnection()
            throws SQLException {
        Connection raw = DriverManager.getConnection(jdbcUrl);
        return new PooledConnection(raw);
    }
}

public class TokenBucketRateLimiter {

    private final int maxTokens;           // bucket capacity
    private final double refillPerNano;    // tokens per nanosecond
    private double availableTokens;
    private long lastRefillNanos;
    private final Object lock = new Object();

    public TokenBucketRateLimiter(int maxTokens,
                                  int refillPerSecond) {
        this.maxTokens = maxTokens;
        this.refillPerNano = refillPerSecond / 1_000_000_000.0;
        this.availableTokens = maxTokens;  // start full
        this.lastRefillNanos = System.nanoTime();
    }

    public boolean tryAcquire() {
        return tryAcquire(1);
    }

    public boolean tryAcquire(int tokens) {
        synchronized (lock) {
            refill();
            if (availableTokens >= tokens) {
                availableTokens -= tokens;
                return true;
            }
            return false; // rejected
        }
    }

    private void refill() {
        long now = System.nanoTime();
        long elapsed = now - lastRefillNanos;
        double newTokens = elapsed * refillPerNano;
        // Cap at maxTokens — never exceed bucket size
        availableTokens = Math.min(maxTokens,
                                    availableTokens + newTokens);
        lastRefillNanos = now;
    }
}

A fixed-window counter resets at every boundary. A client can send 100 requests at 0:59 and 100 more at 1:00, achieving 200 in one second while the per-minute limit is 100. Sliding window fixes this by using overlapping sub-windows.

```java
public class SlidingWindowRateLimiter {

    private final int maxRequests;        // allowed per window
    private final long windowMs;          // e.g., 60_000 for 1 minute
    private final long subWindowMs;       // e.g., 1_000 for 1-second slices
    private final int[] counters;         // circular buffer of sub-windows
    private long currentSubWindow;        // index of current sub-window
    private final Object lock = new Object();

    public SlidingWindowRateLimiter(int maxRequests,
                                    long windowMs,
                                    long subWindowMs) {
        this.maxRequests = maxRequests;
        this.windowMs = windowMs;
        this.subWindowMs = subWindowMs;
        int numSubWindows = (int) (windowMs / subWindowMs);
        this.counters = new int[numSubWindows];
        this.currentSubWindow = System.currentTimeMillis() / subWindowMs;
    }

    public boolean tryAcquire() {
        synchronized (lock) {
            advanceWindow();
            int total = 0;
            for (int c : counters) total += c; // sum all sub-windows

            if (total >= maxRequests) return false;

            counters[currentIndex()]++;
            return true;
        }
    }

    private void advanceWindow() {
        long now = System.currentTimeMillis() / subWindowMs;
        long diff = now - currentSubWindow;
        if (diff > 0) {
            // Zero out stale sub-windows
            for (long i = 0; i < Math.min(diff, counters.length); i++) {
                counters[(int) ((currentSubWindow + i + 1)
                    % counters.length)] = 0;
            }
            currentSubWindow = now;
        }
    }

    private int currentIndex() {
        return (int) (currentSubWindow % counters.length);
    }
}
```

The circular buffer uses modulo indexing so memory is constant regardless of uptime. When the window advances, stale sub-windows are zeroed out. The sum of all sub-windows gives the request count over the last `windowMs` milliseconds — a true sliding window.

In production, each client (identified by API key, IP, or user ID) needs its own rate limiter instance. A `ConcurrentHashMap<String, RateLimiter>` does the job:

```java
private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, TokenBucketRateLimiter>
    limiters = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

public boolean isAllowed(String clientId) {
    return limiters.computeIfAbsent(clientId,
        id -> new TokenBucketRateLimiter(100, 10))
        .tryAcquire();
}
```

**Memory concern:** with millions of clients, each limiter instance consumes heap. Use a background sweeper to evict limiter instances not accessed in the last N minutes, or cap the map size and evict LRU entries.

**Distributed rate limiting:** the code-level limiter works for a single JVM. For multi-node deployments, move state to Redis: `INCR` with `EXPIRE` for fixed-window, Lua scripts for sliding window, or Redis `CL.THROTTLE` from the redis-cell module for token bucket.

**Time complexity:** Token bucket `tryAcquire` is O(1). Sliding window `tryAcquire` is O(w) where w = number of sub-windows (typically 60 or fewer). **Space:** O(c) where c = number of active clients, each storing a small limiter instance.
